WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.180
What if you don't have muscles to do that?

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:05.605
Rachel Tolzman: I love this part [laughs]

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:06.780
where she's like,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.420
"What if you don't have
muscles to do that?"

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.520
And then she's like,

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.560
"All girls have the muscles to do this."

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.703
My name is Rachel Tolzman,

00:00:12.703 --> 00:00:14.900
and I'm a professional pole dancer.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.792
I've been pole dancing for

00:00:16.792 --> 00:00:18.654
probably around eight years,

00:00:18.654 --> 00:00:20.170
and I've competed internationally

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.978
and won a bunch of prestigious titles.

00:00:22.978 --> 00:00:25.246
And today I'm going to be critiquing some

00:00:25.246 --> 00:00:28.240
iconic pole-dancing
scenes in several movies.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.548
OK, so she's doing, like,

00:00:39.548 --> 00:00:42.281
a proper, like, back hook spin there.

00:00:42.281 --> 00:00:44.220
Underarm turn.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.095
Then it kind of cuts out, and I notice

00:00:46.095 --> 00:00:48.010
we only see, like, the
top half of the body,

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.520
and we don't see what's
going on in the bottom half.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.056
I think they make them look more skilled

00:00:54.056 --> 00:00:56.747
with the camera angles that they choose.

00:00:56.747 --> 00:01:00.472
That hand change at the
end was a little bit rocky.

00:01:00.472 --> 00:01:01.749
With the top hand, she doesn't move

00:01:01.749 --> 00:01:03.818
through the twisty-grip position

00:01:03.818 --> 00:01:06.151
and then back naturally
to a true-grip position.

00:01:06.151 --> 00:01:08.931
Like, I think she leaves
her hand position,

00:01:08.931 --> 00:01:11.180
like, in her wrist, in the
same kind of formation,

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.000
and gets, like, stuck there at the end

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.133
and then has to quickly change it,

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:15.716
so it's not very fluid.

00:01:15.716 --> 00:01:18.780
So, there are several
competitions out there

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:19.999
that are highly regulated,

00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:21.890
like the International
Pole Sports Federation.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.187
They send you a booklet full of tricks,

00:01:25.187 --> 00:01:26.750
with the pictures of the tricks

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.680
and how many points are
allotted to that trick

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.418
and how you have to do it

00:01:31.418 --> 00:01:34.042
and how long you have to hold
that specific position for.

00:01:34.042 --> 00:01:36.433
That's, like, very highly technical

00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:39.091
and maybe more sport-like,

00:01:39.091 --> 00:01:40.641
whereas there are other competitions

00:01:40.641 --> 00:01:44.782
that are more theatrical in nature.

00:01:44.782 --> 00:01:47.340
So that requires you to
also be a good storyteller,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.767
good actress, a very good dancer as well.

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:52.820
Like, a seven out of 10.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:53.938
7.5.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.060
So, definitely this is
not Rihanna. [laughs]

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.040
When she does the whole
kind of pass on the pole,

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.740
she does a, well, that's
called a flying K.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.510
And she's doing it in heels,
so that's pretty cool.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:16.286
And then she hooks her outside leg,

00:02:16.286 --> 00:02:18.501
and then switches to an inside leg hang,

00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:21.029
and then kind of reaches back.

00:02:21.029 --> 00:02:22.936
That's called an allegra,

00:02:22.936 --> 00:02:24.944
when she reaches behind the
pole and grabs the other leg,

00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:26.970
and she's trying to
extend it even further.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.723
So it also shows, like,
some flexibility there.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.340
That's just a fan kick.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.140
So, starting from the

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.714
inside leg to the outside leg.

00:02:35.714 --> 00:02:37.056
It takes quite a lot of work

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.097
to hoist your leg up like that,

00:02:39.097 --> 00:02:41.454
that first move she does, the flying K.

00:02:41.454 --> 00:02:44.589
And then to have the confidence
to do it on spin pole.

00:02:44.589 --> 00:02:46.945
So, poles can either
be fixed, like, static,

00:02:46.945 --> 00:02:48.934
or they can be spinning, so that

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:51.091
just a mechanism at
the bottom is released,

00:02:51.091 --> 00:02:53.932
and the pole rotates.

00:02:53.932 --> 00:02:56.057
If you compete, then you are expected

00:02:56.057 --> 00:03:00.047
to do some of your passes on static pole

00:03:00.047 --> 00:03:03.254
and some of your other elements on spin.

00:03:03.254 --> 00:03:05.045
Mainly if you're doing any sort of, like,

00:03:05.045 --> 00:03:06.570
backflips or things like that,

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.810
you're gonna be doing it on a fixed pole.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.943
So you'd be doing it on the static one.

00:03:10.943 --> 00:03:11.920
Eight out of 10.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.253
Almost at an advanced level.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.831
What an entrance. Like, she's [laughs]

00:03:22.831 --> 00:03:24.558
already, like, approaching the stage

00:03:24.558 --> 00:03:26.110
and the pole with such confidence.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.673
So, she goes into, like, a basic spin

00:03:28.673 --> 00:03:30.830
that everybody learns
when they begin pole,

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.496
so it's a chair spin,

00:03:32.496 --> 00:03:34.921
and then she goes into, like, a back hook.

00:03:34.921 --> 00:03:37.010
And body waves. I mean, she has this down,

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:37.901
you know. [laughs]

00:03:37.901 --> 00:03:39.030
Body rolls.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.476
Front-hook spin, maybe fish-hook spin,

00:03:42.476 --> 00:03:44.030
I can't really tell 'cause
they cut off her legs.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.227
Now it's, like, a fairy sit,

00:03:46.227 --> 00:03:47.820
and coming down.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.010
Oh, here's where she inverts,

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:50.931
and that's really actually quite great.

00:03:50.931 --> 00:03:53.082
However, she does use her outside leg

00:03:53.082 --> 00:03:54.700
to kind of hoist herself up on the pole,

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.023
but, I mean, it's amazing, what she does.

00:03:57.023 --> 00:04:01.022
Sliding down on the pole
is a little bit like...

00:04:01.022 --> 00:04:04.120
I don't know, like how she
was taught to come down,

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.240
but it looks very nice.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.840
Actually, so, she's crossing her legs

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.140
almost like a hang back.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.330
However, it's sort of like a mix

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.527
between a hang back and a crucifix,

00:04:14.527 --> 00:04:16.431
because she's keeping her inside shoulder

00:04:16.431 --> 00:04:17.400
in front of the pole.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.896
If you can't come down from an invert

00:04:19.896 --> 00:04:22.250
in, like, a forwards
position, in a controlled way,

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:23.820
then it kind of looks kind of clunky.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.125
And that just requires more strength

00:04:26.125 --> 00:04:27.700
and probably more time than she had

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.410
to prepare for this role.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.822
But she actually does invert,
so that's pretty cool.

00:04:32.822 --> 00:04:34.728
There are so many dollar bills. I'm like,

00:04:34.728 --> 00:04:37.045
I'm pretty sure that that
never really happens,

00:04:37.045 --> 00:04:39.086
that you have, like, that amount of money

00:04:39.086 --> 00:04:41.177
being thrown at you. [laughs] I mean,

00:04:41.177 --> 00:04:43.070
I think more people would
be doing it otherwise,

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:44.310
you know?

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.226
Ramona: Hold on with that knee.

00:04:45.226 --> 00:04:46.276
Destiny: OK.

00:04:46.276 --> 00:04:48.744
Rachel: I really, really like this scene,

00:04:48.744 --> 00:04:51.934
because it does actually kind of showcase

00:04:51.934 --> 00:04:55.642
somebody, like, learning the very first

00:04:55.642 --> 00:04:57.433
basic moves in pole.

00:04:57.433 --> 00:04:58.950
How you can hook your legs on the pole,

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.238
how can you hook your ankle on the pole.

00:05:01.238 --> 00:05:03.749
We sort of have, like, a common vocabulary

00:05:03.749 --> 00:05:07.641
in the pole world with names of tricks.

00:05:07.641 --> 00:05:11.485
Some of them vary slightly,
depending on where you are.

00:05:11.485 --> 00:05:13.720
Ramona: Here you could do a carousel.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.360
Rachel: So, I would
call this a chair spin.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.360
To me, a carousel spin is when your hips

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.255
are actually facing the pole
the whole time in the spin,

00:05:20.255 --> 00:05:22.999
whereas the chair spin,
you're spinning to the side

00:05:22.999 --> 00:05:24.548
and you're basically

00:05:24.548 --> 00:05:26.400
parallel to the pole as you go around.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.100
Ramona: Go into a fairy sit.
Rachel: Fairy sit, yeah.

00:05:31.022 --> 00:05:32.010
Ramona: Stag.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.945
Rachel: Well, so, we call this a stag,

00:05:33.945 --> 00:05:36.493
or double stag, or sun wheel as well.

00:05:37.426 --> 00:05:39.674
Here she does a,

00:05:39.674 --> 00:05:42.012
I would say that's a
layout, not a tabletop,

00:05:42.012 --> 00:05:43.506
but [laughs].

00:05:43.506 --> 00:05:45.467
Destiny: What if you don't
have muscles to do that?

00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:46.300
[laughs]

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:47.133
Rachel: Where she's like,

00:05:47.133 --> 00:05:48.760
"What if you don't have
muscles to do that?"

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:49.835
And then she's like,

00:05:49.835 --> 00:05:51.670
"All girls have the muscles to do this."

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:54.170
Like, it's just so, it's
a lovely moment, I think,

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:56.068
that they showcase here in this scene,

00:05:56.068 --> 00:05:58.960
because this is just
like the typical journey

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.920
that everyone takes when
they start learning pole.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:04.782
And, you know, she has these
probably 7-inch heels on,

00:06:04.782 --> 00:06:06.270
so she's already starting

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.620
from an elevated point on the floor,

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.440
so when she does do the two climbs,

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:11.850
it looks like she's quite high.

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:15.602
The viewer probably may
come to a realization,

00:06:15.602 --> 00:06:18.039
"Oh, wow, well actually this is really,

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:21.096
like, can be very technical
and is a lot of hard work."

00:06:21.096 --> 00:06:23.104
I would give a nine out of 10.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.750
I think they're purposely
doing a really poor job

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.263
[laughs] of anything pole-related.

00:06:35.263 --> 00:06:36.820
There's a competition called Pole Theatre,

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.070
and there's a comedic category,

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:41.566
so you can enter and do
something funny like this,

00:06:41.566 --> 00:06:43.576
and the purpose is to
make people laugh, so.

00:06:43.576 --> 00:06:44.990
They're trying to do body waves here,

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:45.880
but they're doing, like,

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.255
these strange undulations.

00:06:49.255 --> 00:06:50.230
[laughs]

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.243
This is like, very poor
example of, like, "Magic Mike."

00:06:53.243 --> 00:06:54.780
The hand position is always so weird.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.103
Like, why would anyone grab here?

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.180
I would not advise licking the pole.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.479
Yeah, that's kinda gross. [laughs]

00:07:05.479 --> 00:07:07.950
Like, a 1.5.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:09.538
Yeah.

00:07:09.538 --> 00:07:10.751
Sorry, Steve.

00:07:17.973 --> 00:07:19.089
The arching back, I mean,

00:07:19.089 --> 00:07:20.789
it's so common in some of these clips.

00:07:20.789 --> 00:07:22.910
It's just, like, throwing
yourself in a backbend.

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.634
Like, OK.

00:07:24.634 --> 00:07:26.160
This, I was really impressed with.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.960
It's kind of like a box split

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.177
that she's sliding down into,

00:07:29.177 --> 00:07:31.197
so she has great flexibility.

00:07:31.197 --> 00:07:33.238
Her back hook spin is really nice

00:07:33.238 --> 00:07:35.266
and really fluid at the
end where she kind of,

00:07:35.266 --> 00:07:36.998
kind of does, like, a little arch up.

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:38.925
And here is where she's doing an invert,

00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:40.492
and she comes into, like, almost like a

00:07:40.492 --> 00:07:43.229
caterpillar climb, like, body wave.

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:44.930
First you invert, you go upside down,

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:46.880
you go into what's called a crucifix.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.100
So, that means your legs
are above your head.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.364
And then you proceed to
climb the pole upside down.

00:07:52.364 --> 00:07:54.038
And the reason why it's
called a caterpillar

00:07:54.038 --> 00:07:56.532
is because you're kind of, like,

00:07:56.532 --> 00:07:58.295
if you go up the pole like that,

00:07:58.295 --> 00:08:00.096
it kind of looks like
a little bit of a wave,

00:08:00.096 --> 00:08:03.080
like a little worm or a
little caterpillar going up.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:04.714
Yeah, she's not moving with the climb,

00:08:04.714 --> 00:08:07.348
she's just holding with
in between her knees.

00:08:07.348 --> 00:08:09.620
And there's not a lot going on,

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:12.120
but the elements that do happen, I mean,

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.982
it's, like, a seven.

00:08:21.183 --> 00:08:25.748
Little bit of sassy kind
of, like, hip movement.

00:08:25.748 --> 00:08:26.970
This is definitely, I think,

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:28.749
along the lines of more of a striptease,

00:08:28.749 --> 00:08:31.596
burlesque, rather than anything pole.

00:08:31.596 --> 00:08:34.611
There was sort of a step around.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.350
Pole dance can be so
many different things.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:46.080
It is, I think it is an art form.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.782
I think it is a sport as well.

00:08:47.782 --> 00:08:49.807
So, one could argue that this is just

00:08:49.807 --> 00:08:51.690
highlighting kind of one aspect

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:54.060
or one side of the pole world.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.800
The thing that comes to
people's mind the most

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.400
when they instantly think of pole,

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.700
if they don't know otherwise,

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.773
maybe they kind of assume that it

00:09:02.773 --> 00:09:05.389
is something that belongs
in the exotic world.

00:09:05.389 --> 00:09:09.098
The art form evolved from different forms,

00:09:09.098 --> 00:09:10.899
coming from India and China first.

00:09:10.899 --> 00:09:13.799
So, we have men on poles
doing acrobatic tricks

00:09:13.799 --> 00:09:15.340
and jumping from pole to pole.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:16.912
And so you have that element,

00:09:16.912 --> 00:09:18.787
but then, of course,

00:09:18.787 --> 00:09:21.245
the more burlesque kind of side of pole

00:09:21.245 --> 00:09:25.250
evolved in circuses and circus tents

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:28.420
and ladies, you know,
dancing around the pole,

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.580
and then, you know, doing
a little burlesque act.

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:31.663
And so that's kind of where

00:09:31.663 --> 00:09:34.305
that side of pole originated.

00:09:34.305 --> 00:09:38.764
She's very believable as
that kind of character.

00:09:38.764 --> 00:09:42.050
But technically, maybe like a two.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:43.519
So. [laughs]

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:52.007
For some reason, reminds me
a little bit of "Flashdance,"

00:09:52.007 --> 00:09:53.750
but it's not as nice, you know. [laughs]

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:54.583
It's like...[laughs]

00:09:54.583 --> 00:09:56.070
That was so bad.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:59.453
Sorry, I don't mean any
disrespect to the actress.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.980
But, like, basically
looks like she was trying

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.008
to do a fireman spin.

00:10:05.008 --> 00:10:07.495
A fireman spin is where you have the pole,

00:10:07.495 --> 00:10:08.490
like, in between the thighs,

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:11.349
and your feet are
pointed towards your bum.

00:10:11.349 --> 00:10:12.748
But it's just like, she just, like,

00:10:12.748 --> 00:10:14.490
jumps onto the pole.

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:17.010
First teaching people how to
do spins and basic things,

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:18.676
I was like, "No jumping." [laughs]

00:10:18.676 --> 00:10:20.726
You're not allowed to just kind of

00:10:20.726 --> 00:10:22.743
jump onto the pole.

00:10:22.743 --> 00:10:25.840
And then a very aggressive striptease.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:29.233
Some sort of, like, step-around spin.

00:10:29.233 --> 00:10:31.220
More licking the pole! [laughs]

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:32.980
This is the worst.

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:35.083
That one, like, little
turn she does with her leg

00:10:35.083 --> 00:10:36.840
out to the side, it looks quite nice.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:40.240
I think that's the best
move she does here.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.249
I would give her an A
for effort, but [laughs]

00:10:43.249 --> 00:10:47.485
I would probably, 4.5.

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:58.018
Well, it is highly
choreographed, first of all.

00:10:58.018 --> 00:11:00.868
Kind of jazzy elements in it,

00:11:00.868 --> 00:11:03.500
and also, oh, there, like, Latin dance.

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:04.523
Like, ballroom.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:11.508
[laughs] The heels on the poles.

00:11:11.508 --> 00:11:13.326
I don't know, it just kinda reminds me

00:11:13.326 --> 00:11:15.010
of something like Fosse.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:16.026
I don't know why, like...

00:11:16.026 --> 00:11:17.542
yeah, just the clacking of the heels,

00:11:17.542 --> 00:11:18.640
and then the stomping.

00:11:21.529 --> 00:11:22.820
Then some acro tricks.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:26.080
OK, so these guys now are
doing proper handsprings.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:28.600
So, that's what that move is called.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:30.380
And they're just hanging
up there in, like,

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:32.063
a pike position or something.

00:11:32.063 --> 00:11:33.162
And then they do, like,

00:11:33.162 --> 00:11:35.833
a little middle split leap. I love it.

00:11:35.833 --> 00:11:37.025
Nice, dramatic ending,

